import ProductionLayout from '../layouts/ProductionLayout.astro';
---
<ProductionLayout title="The System — Skyhook Industries" description="How the Skyhook transportation concept works: orbital rotation, moving-tip rendezvous, momentum exchange, tether structure, reboost and failure management.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">The system / MXT study</p>
    <h1>A machine in two motions.</h1>
    <p>The Skyhook orbits Earth while rotating around its own center of mass. That combination creates the handoff: a lower tip that can momentarily move much more slowly than the orbital center, and an upper tip that moves faster.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="dark-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">01 / Geometry</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Orbit and rotation are inseparable.</h2>
        <p class="body-copy">The tether is not anchored to Earth and it does not hang motionless over one point. Its center of mass follows an orbit while the structure rotates. Pickup altitude, center-of-mass altitude, rotation period, tether length and lower-tip velocity therefore have to be solved together.</p>
        <div class="mechanism">
          <div class="figure">
            <div class="figure-head"><span>Fig. 02 / Coupled motions</span><span>Concept — not to scale</span></div>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 560 430" role="img" aria-label="Skyhook orbital and rotational motion diagram">
              <defs><marker id="sArrow" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="7" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z" fill="#d9e9ed"/></marker></defs>
              <circle cx="280" cy="365" r="145" fill="#0b1a23" stroke="#284554"/><ellipse cx="280" cy="230" rx="220" ry="92" fill="none" stroke="#455860" stroke-dasharray="6 8"/>
              <g transform="rotate(-22 280 188)"><line x1="133" y1="330" x2="444" y2="38" stroke="#f1f6f7" stroke-width="4"/><circle cx="280" cy="188" r="8" fill="#f1f6f7"/><circle cx="133" cy="330" r="6" fill="#d9e9ed"/><circle cx="444" cy="38" r="6" fill="#d9e9ed"/></g>
              <path d="M350 117 C405 104 447 75 474 34" fill="none" stroke="#d9e9ed" marker-end="url(#sArrow)"/><path d="M78 232 C91 194 119 165 159 146" fill="none" stroke="#79909a" marker-end="url(#sArrow)"/>
              <text x="359" y="98" fill="#9dafb6" font-family="monospace" font-size="12">ROTATION</text><text x="53" y="216" fill="#7f939c" font-family="monospace" font-size="12">ORBIT</text><text x="293" y="181" fill="#9dafb6" font-family="monospace" font-size="12">COM</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div class="body-copy"><p>For the current study, “~4,000 km” means the physical tether length class, not a claim that every final design will use exactly that span. A symmetric two-ended configuration is attractive because it avoids assuming a separate multi-million-ton counterweight, but payload capture still changes the system’s center of mass and rotational state.</p><p>That makes mass distribution and post-capture control part of the architecture, not details to be added later.</p></div>
        </div>

        <div class="scale-reference" aria-label="Same-scale comparison of Earth diameter and the current Skyhook tether length target">
          <div class="figure-head"><span>Fig. 03 / Scale reference</span><span>Physical lengths / same scale</span></div>
          <div class="scale-row">
            <div class="scale-meta"><b>EARTH</b><span>Mean diameter</span><strong>12,742 km</strong></div>
            <div class="scale-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="scale-bar earth"></span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="scale-row">
            <div class="scale-meta"><b>SKYHOOK STUDY</b><span>Physical tether length</span><strong>~4,000 km</strong></div>
            <div class="scale-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="scale-bar tether"></span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="scale-note">Scale comparison only. This is not an orbital-geometry diagram and does not depict a ground connection.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="architecture-readout">
          <div class="figure-head"><span>Fig. 04 / First numerical closure</span><span>Reference Architecture v0.3</span></div>
          <p class="body-copy">A first screening model now exists behind the study length. It assumes two equal 2,000 km arms, a 100 km lower-tip altitude at the radial pass, and a circular center-of-mass orbit. That puts the center of mass at about 2,100 km altitude and the opposite tip at about 4,100 km.</p>
          <div class="sequence compact-sequence">
            <div class="sequence-row"><b>3.0</b><strong>km/s lower tip</strong><span>54.3 min rotation / 10.72 km/s upper tip / 10.88 MJ/kg lower-arm self-load index</span></div>
            <div class="sequence-row"><b>4.1</b><strong>km/s lower tip</strong><span>75.9 min rotation / 9.62 km/s upper tip / 7.24 MJ/kg lower-arm self-load index</span></div>
            <div class="sequence-row"><b>5.0</b><strong>km/s lower tip</strong><span>112.6 min rotation / 8.72 km/s upper tip / 5.16 MJ/kg lower-arm self-load index</span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="body-copy">The 4.1 km/s row is included because Boeing’s HASTOL Phase I study used a 100 km / 4.1 km/s inertial rendezvous in a very different 600 km architecture. Skyhook Industries has not adopted that vehicle speed as a requirement. The sensitivity is the point: making the lower tip easier to reach forces the tether to rotate harder, which raises structural load quickly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="paper-band">
    <section class="paper-section">
      <div class="section-grid">
        <p class="chapter-label">02 / Capture</p>
        <div>
          <h2>The handoff lasts seconds, not minutes.</h2>
          <p class="lede">A payload cannot loiter next to a rotating tether and dock at leisure. The lower end arrives at the rendezvous point on a clock.</p>
          <div class="fact-table">
            <div class="fact-row"><b>Navigation</b><p>Vehicle and tether state estimates must converge tightly enough that the payload enters the capture volume at the right position, velocity and attitude.</p><small>Open subsystem</small></div>
            <div class="fact-row"><b>Capture envelope</b><p>The mechanism must tolerate realistic closing error rather than requiring mathematical perfection.</p><small>Open subsystem</small></div>
            <div class="fact-row"><b>Load transfer</b><p>Capture converts relative motion into structural load. Shock management and controlled load transfer are central design problems.</p><small>Open subsystem</small></div>
            <div class="fact-row"><b>Abort</b><p>A missed or unhealthy capture must leave both vehicle and tether in survivable trajectories.</p><small>Required behavior</small></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="paper-section">
      <div class="section-grid">
        <p class="chapter-label">03 / Momentum exchange</p>
        <div>
          <h2>The payload gains what the tether gives up.</h2>
          <p class="lede">There is no free energy in the handoff. Accelerating the payload costs the tether orbital energy and momentum. That loss is exactly why a reusable system needs recovery between missions.</p>
          <p class="body-copy">A useful design is therefore sized around more than a dramatic tip velocity. Payload mass, tether mass, release geometry, allowable orbit change and recovery time all determine throughput. The engineering question is not “can one payload be thrown?” but “can the facility do it repeatedly without drifting out of its operating envelope?”</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="dark-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">04 / Recovery</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Rebuild the orbit between missions.</h2>
        <p class="body-copy">Electrodynamic reboost is a leading candidate because a conductor moving through Earth’s magnetic field can exchange momentum with the environment through Lorentz forces. Electrical power can therefore restore orbital energy without carrying conventional reaction mass for that reboost function.</p>
        <div class="sequence">
          <div class="sequence-row"><b>A</b><strong>PAYLOAD RELEASE</strong><span>Tether orbit and rotational state are lower-energy than before capture.</span></div>
          <div class="sequence-row"><b>B</b><strong>POWER</strong><span>Solar arrays or another electrical source drive the recovery system.</span></div>
          <div class="sequence-row"><b>C</b><strong>CURRENT</strong><span>The electrodynamic element interacts with ionospheric plasma and Earth’s magnetic field.</span></div>
          <div class="sequence-row"><b>D</b><strong>RESTORE</strong><span>The facility climbs back toward its operational state before the next mission.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="body-copy">This does not make the complete transportation chain “fuel free.” The vehicle arriving from Earth still needs propulsion, and the recovery rate depends on orbit, magnetic-field geometry, current collection, conductor design, power and plasma conditions.</p>
        <p class="body-copy">The v0.3 screening model also puts a scale on the recovery problem. In the deliberately aggressive 4.1 km/s full lower-to-upper transfer, the payload gains about 61.4 MJ/kg of Earth-relative mechanical energy. Recovering that in one day would require an ideal average of about 0.71 MW per metric tonne of payload before losses. Earlier release or longer recovery time reduces the requirement.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="paper-band">
    <section class="paper-section">
      <div class="section-grid">
        <p class="chapter-label">05 / Structure</p>
        <div>
          <h2>Four thousand kilometres cannot behave like one rope.</h2>
          <p class="lede">The current structural direction is tapered, redundant and sectional: more material where the load demands it, multiple load paths where damage is credible, and boundaries that help keep a local failure local.</p>
          <div class="hard-list">
            <div class="hard-row"><b>TAPER</b><span>CROSS-SECTION FOLLOWS LOAD / MASS IS NOT UNIFORM / PEAK STRESS MUST SET GEOMETRY</span></div>
            <div class="hard-row"><b>REDUNDANCY</b><span>MULTIPLE LOAD PATHS / SPATIALLY SEPARATED ELEMENTS / DAMAGE TOLERANCE</span></div>
            <div class="hard-row"><b>SECTIONS</b><span>INSPECTION / REPLACEMENT / ISOLATION / CONTROLLED FAILURE BOUNDARIES</span></div>
            <div class="hard-row"><b>MATERIAL</b><span>ALLOWABLE SPECIFIC STRENGTH AFTER JOINTS / FATIGUE / ENVIRONMENT / SAFETY FACTOR</span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="body-copy">For the 4.1 km/s comparison case, distributed self-load alone gives an ideal lower-arm center-to-tip taper ratio of about 6.1× at 4 MJ/kg allowable specific strength, 3.3× at 6 MJ/kg and 2.5× at 8 MJ/kg. Those are not mass estimates: payload tension, capture hardware, joints, redundancy and damage allowance still have to set the absolute section size.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="paper-section">
      <div class="section-grid">
        <p class="chapter-label">06 / Failure</p>
        <div>
          <h2>Design the broken machine too.</h2>
          <p class="lede">Micrometeoroids, orbital debris, thermal cycling, atomic oxygen, fatigue and deployment faults are not edge cases for a tether. Survivability has to be part of the initial architecture.</p>
          <p class="body-copy">Deliberate severing or sectional isolation may eventually help prevent one local failure from becoming a full-length event, but cutting a tether is not automatically safe. Every credible sever location has to be propagated forward: fragment orbit, collision probability, lifetime and eventual re-entry all matter.</p>
          <a class="cta light" href="/roadmap/">See how the program closes these risks →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</ProductionLayout>
